<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">First French Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Napoleonic Empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> was the state ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> from his coronation as emperor in 1804 until 1814, with a brief return in 1815. At its height around 1811 it annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and dominated most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through allied and client states before defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> brought its fall.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The empire grew out of the Consulate. On 9 November 1799 troops led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> seized control in a coup planned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès</a>, and Bonaparte outmaneuvered Sieyès to become First Consul. He was made First Consul for life in 1802, after a plebiscite that gave 3,653,600 votes in favour and 8,272 against. On 18 May 1804 the Senate gave him the title Emperor of the French, and on 2 December 1804 he was crowned in Notre-Dame de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a>. The state nevertheless continued to be formally called the French Republic until October 1808.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-third-coalition">War of the Third Coalition</a> the French army won a string of victories over Austrian and Russian forces, most notably at Austerlitz in 1805. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, and on 6 August 1806 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> abdicated the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. He destroyed the Prussian armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Auerstedt, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a> removed about half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s territory. To build satellite states he installed relatives as rulers, among them his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, later King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, strained the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the seizure of the Spanish throne provoked a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Austria was defeated at Wagram in 1809 and ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a>. In 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded Russia and lost nearly his whole army. After defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1813, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> capitulated on 30 March 1814, and Napoleon abdicated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> on 11 April. He returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> and revived the empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a> of 1815, but was defeated at Waterloo and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a>, where he died in 1821.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At its height in 1812 the empire had 130 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and a population of over 44 million, and ruled some 90 million subjects across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a> increased legal equality, established jury systems and legalised divorce, while seigneurial dues and justice were abolished, as were aristocratic privileges everywhere except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Napoleon preserved many social gains of the Revolution while suppressing political liberty: he rejected electoral democracy and freedom of the press, though his rule included public education and the emancipation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First French Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The powers of the Senate grew under the Constitution of the Year X. On 18 May 1804 the Conservative Senate proclaimed Napoleon Emperor of the French, ending the First Republic and opening the <b>First French Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-first-republic">French First Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
